\documentclass{article}
\begin{document}
\section{Introduction}
Long-document question answering strains both the retrieval and the
decoding budgets of a standard pipeline.
Our pipeline for pivot-guided retrieval makes three contributions:
\begin{itemize}
\item A pivot-aware encoder that compresses retrieved passages.
\item A reranking stage that trades latency for recall.
\item A decoding strategy that reduces hallucination on long contexts.
\end{itemize}

\section{Terminology}
We use the following terms throughout the paper.
\begin{description}
\item[Pivot] The anchor span that retrieval and reranking condition on.
\item[Retrieval pool] The candidate passages returned by the first-stage index.
\item[Reranker] A cross-encoder that reorders the retrieval pool.
\item[Token budget] The maximum context length passed to the decoder.
\end{description}

\section{Method}
The encoder first extracts pivot spans from the question and the
document title.
Retrieval then conditions on the pivot representation rather than the
raw question, which reduces sensitivity to phrasing.
The reranker scores each passage against the pivot and the question
jointly, and the decoder consumes only the top passages that fit the
token budget.

\section{Conclusion}
Pivot-guided retrieval is a simple change to the standard pipeline
that improves robustness on long documents without extra training
data.
\end{document}

